+### Cron requests and HTTP 429
+
+The frontend and backend run in the same Flask service. Use its public
+`GET /health` endpoint for cron requests. It returns HTTP `200` with
+`{"status":"ok"}` without authentication, database queries, redirects, or
+external API calls. It also supports `HEAD` and sets `Cache-Control: no-store`.
+This checks the web process; it does not check database availability.
+
+After deploying this change, configure one external cron job:
+
+- URL: your service's HTTPS URL followed by `/health` (no trailing slash),
+  e.g. `https://fanpage-ez7l.onrender.com/health`.
+- Method: `GET`.
+- Schedule: every 10 minutes (`*/10 * * * *`).
+- Timeout: at least 90 seconds, if the cron provider supports it, to allow an
+  idle service to start. Avoid overlapping jobs and immediate retries.
+
+For a Blueprint-managed service, sync `render.yaml`. For an existing service
+created manually, update its Start Command and Health Check Path to the values
+in Step 3. Render's own health checks do not configure an external cron job.
+
+Check the endpoint once after deployment:
+
+```bash
+curl --include --max-time 90 https://YOUR-SERVICE.onrender.com/health
+```
+
+A missing `/health` route returns `404`. The application rate limiter below
+always exempts `/health`; adding a health route cannot remove a rate limit
+imposed before a request reaches Flask. Render documents inbound throttling,
+especially for free services, as one possible cause of `429`:
+[Render's HTTP error guide](https://render.com/docs/troubleshooting-deploys#429-too-many-requests).
+
+If `429` continues, capture the cron response headers/body and timestamp, check
+any `Retry-After` header, and compare one manual request with one scheduled
+request. The Gunicorn start command logs requests to Render's service logs.
+A rejected request with no matching access log suggests it was blocked before
+reaching this app; investigate Render, any proxy, and the cron provider. Cron
+requests remain subject to [Render's free-plan limits](https://render.com/docs/free).
+
+### API rate limits
+
+Flask-Limiter limits API traffic by client IP. The defaults are:
+
+| Requests | Limit per IP | Environment variable |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| All API endpoints combined | 120 per minute | `RATELIMIT_APPLICATION` |
+| Folder/video POST, PATCH, and DELETE requests combined | 30 per minute | `RATELIMIT_API_WRITES` |
+| `POST /api/admin/verify`, including failed attempts | 5 per minute | `RATELIMIT_ADMIN_VERIFY` |
+
+The limits apply together. Exceeding one returns HTTP `429` with a JSON error,
+`Retry-After` in seconds, and `X-RateLimit-*` headers. The browser displays the
+error rather than retrying automatically. The homepage, static assets, uploaded
+cover images, and `/health` (including HEAD requests) never use these quotas.
+
+The default store is `memory://`; the start commands explicitly run one Gunicorn
+worker so requests share its counters. Counts reset on restart. Before increasing
+the worker count or adding service instances, set `RATELIMIT_STORAGE_URI` to a
+shared Redis URL (`redis://...` or `rediss://...`); Redis support is included in
+`requirements.txt`. Do not put a
+Redis password in source control. See [Flask-Limiter storage configuration](https://flask-limiter.readthedocs.io/en/stable/configuration.html#ratelimit-storage-uri).
+
+On Render (`RENDER=true`), client identification uses a single validated
+`CF-Connecting-IP`, based on Render's public traffic passing through Cloudflare.
+This assumes the public Render edge supplies that header; if it is missing or
+invalid, the limiter uses the socket address instead. Arbitrary
+`X-Forwarded-For` values are ignored. Locally, forwarded headers are ignored by
+default. Set `RATELIMIT_TRUST_CLOUDFLARE=false` to disable this trust, or enable
+it only behind a Cloudflare edge you control. See [Render's edge architecture](https://render.com/articles/how-render-handles-ddos-attacks)
+and [Cloudflare's header documentation](https://developers.cloudflare.com/fundamentals/reference/http-headers/).
+
+Run the regression checks with:
+
+```bash
+.venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
+```
+
